      "fraud_reported": "the label itself.",
      "claim_id": "surrogate key, no generalizable signal; a tree could memorize it.",
      "incident_id": "surrogate key, same reason.",
      "policy_number": "natural key, same reason.",
      "vehicle_id": "surrogate key, same reason.",
      "insured_zip": "995 distinct values across 1,000 rows -- functionally an identifier, not a generalizable category; including it risks memorizing individual policyholders rather than learning a pattern.",
      "incident_city": "high-cardinality free text; same identifier risk as insured_zip.",
      "incident_location": "a street address -- uniquely identifying, not a repeatable pattern.",
      "auto_model": "39 values spread across only 14 auto_make values and 1,000 rows -- too sparse to generalize; auto_make alone already captures the make-level signal found in the SQL analysis (ANALYSIS.md F5).",
      "injury_claim": "a component of total_claim_amount (injury + property + vehicle = total, verified in data/normalize_data.py). Including the components alongside the total adds perfect collinearity, not new information.",
      "property_claim": "same reason as injury_claim.",
      "vehicle_claim": "same reason as injury_claim.",
      "policy_bind_date": "raw date; tenure is already captured by months_as_customer. The SQL analysis (Q3) found no usable signal from the exact bind date.",
      "incident_date": "raw date; the data only spans ~2 months (ANALYSIS.md F6/Q8), so a date-derived feature would overfit to that narrow window rather than learn anything that generalizes.",
      "insured_hobbies": "EXCLUDED FROM THE PRODUCTION MODEL DESPITE BEING THE SINGLE STRONGEST UNIVARIATE PREDICTOR FOUND. 'chess' -> 82.6% fraud (n=46) and 'cross-fit' -> 74.3% fraud (n=35), versus <=30% for every other one of 20 hobby values and a 24.7% portfolio base rate. There is no real-world causal story connecting a hobby to fraud at that magnitude; this is a well-known artifact of this specific public dataset. Using it would make the model look far more accurate than any model built on real claims data could be. A sensitivity run WITH it included is still produced (see train_model.py --with-hobbies) purely to document the effect size, never as a deployed feature."
    }
  },
